  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "NMA-Q01",
      "title": "Arpeggiator Hold replacement versus physical-held additions",
      "blocks": [
        "NME:B020"
      ],
      "assessment": "Existing010 release-withoutnewkey and014allkeysup U remain. New held-key additions must not resolve all-keys-up replacement.",
      "documentary_expected": null,
      "documentary_comparison": null,
      "actual_expected": null,
      "actual_comparison": null,
      "target": "U",
      "executed": false,
      "complete": false,
      "quotes": [
        {
          "block": "NME:B020",
          "json_pointer": "/blocks/19/text",
          "quote": "When playing notes using a MIDI controller, you can enable the Hold switch to keep the pattern playing even after releasing the keys. The pattern will continue to repeat until another key is pressed. You can hold an initial set of keys and then press additional keys to add notes to a currently held pattern. To remove notes, play them a second time. This allows you to create a gradual buildup and rearrangement of the pattern over time."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "NMA-Q02",
      "title": "Random Alt conflicting cardinalities",
      "blocks": [
        "NME:B079",
        "NME:B080"
      ],
      "assessment": "Retain exact12/1C3..C4 versus2/2C3,D3 examples and050/051U, no general cycle/cardinality/phase.",
      "documentary_expected": null,
      "documentary_comparison": null,
      "actual_expected": null,
      "actual_comparison": null,
      "target": "U",
      "executed": false,
      "complete": false,
      "quotes": [
        {
          "block": "NME:B079",
          "json_pointer": "/blocks/78/text",
          "quote": "Mode determines how the randomization occurs: Random assigns pitches in no particular order, while Alt cycles through pitches in a fixed round-robin order. The Chance control behaves differently in this mode: at 100%, the next output note will always be the next note in the sequence, while at 0%, the next output note will always be the incoming note."
        },
        {
          "block": "NME:B080",
          "json_pointer": "/blocks/79/text",
          "quote": "For example, when Chance is set to 100%, Choices is set to 12, and Interval is set to 1, playing C3 once plays C3, and each successive C3 plays the next semitone higher until C4, at which point the cycle starts over from C3. When Chance is set to 100% and Choices and Interval are set to 2, incoming C3s alternate between C3 and D3. This behavior is ideal for simulating upbow and downbow alternation with stringed instruments, or alternating right- and left-hand drum samples."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "NMA-Q03",
      "title": "Note Off latch release/pedal transition",
      "blocks": [
        "NME:B064"
      ],
      "assessment": "039U;037OnLatch and038disabledcontrols do not prove which newoff qualifies. CorrectedNL-L01/L05LatchOFF retained.",
      "documentary_expected": null,
      "documentary_comparison": null,
      "actual_expected": null,
      "actual_comparison": null,
      "target": "U",
      "executed": false,
      "complete": false,
      "quotes": [
        {
          "block": "NME:B064",
          "json_pointer": "/blocks/63/text",
          "quote": "You can enable the Latch toggle to activate note latching. When using Note On messages, triggered notes continue playing until the next Note On message is received. When using Note Off messages, notes are triggered once all keys (or a sustain pedal, if connected) are released and a new Note Off message is received. In both cases, the Gate and Length controls are deactivated, as the length of triggered notes depends on the Note On/Off messages. Additionally, the Release Velocity, Decay Time, and Key Scale controls are deactivated for Note Off messages."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "NMA-Q04",
      "title": "Pitch Range UI unit/upper endpoint",
      "blocks": [
        "NME:B069",
        "NME:B070",
        "NME:B071",
        "NME:B072",
        "NME:B073"
      ],
      "assessment": "R2review002 uses acceptedpositiveRange/no unit. Lowerbound044 is true, no arbitrarywidth/upperendpoint/Foldalgorithm.",
      "documentary_expected": null,
      "documentary_comparison": null,
      "actual_expected": null,
      "actual_comparison": null,
      "target": "U",
      "executed": false,
      "complete": false,
      "quotes": [
        {
          "block": "NME:B069",
          "json_pointer": "/blocks/68/text",
          "quote": "The Lowest and Range controls define the pitch range through which notes are allowed to pass through the effect."
        },
        {
          "block": "NME:B070",
          "json_pointer": "/blocks/69/text",
          "quote": "You can select an option in the Mode chooser to determine how notes outside the set range are processed:"
        },
        {
          "block": "NME:B071",
          "json_pointer": "/blocks/70/text",
          "quote": "In Block mode, notes outside the range are blocked from passing through. The LED next to the Range control flashes when this happens."
        },
        {
          "block": "NME:B072",
          "json_pointer": "/blocks/71/text",
          "quote": "In Fold mode, notes outside the range are transposed to fit within the range."
        },
        {
          "block": "NME:B073",
          "json_pointer": "/blocks/72/text",
          "quote": "In Limit mode, notes are restricted to the range. This means that any notes below the lowest note in the range are transposed up to that note, and any notes above the highest note in the range are transposed down to fit."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "NMA-Q05",
      "title": "Velocity Random displacement baseline",
      "blocks": [
        "NME:B107"
      ],
      "assessment": "R2review003/064 retain60..127membership only; rawvsunrandomizedmappedbaseline unresolved, no up/down50 target oracle.",
      "documentary_expected": null,
      "documentary_comparison": null,
      "actual_expected": null,
      "actual_comparison": null,
      "target": "U",
      "executed": false,
      "complete": false,
      "quotes": [
        {
          "block": "NME:B107",
          "json_pointer": "/blocks/106/text",
          "quote": "Random introduces random modulation to all incoming velocities. The range of modulation is determined by the set value and is influenced by the Output Range controls. For example, when Out Hi is set to 127 and Out Low is set to 60, a Random value of 50 shifts the velocities up or down by up to 50 within the range of 60-127."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "NMA-Q06",
      "title": "Scale13x13 versus12notes",
      "blocks": [
        "NME:B092",
        "NME:B093",
        "NME:B094"
      ],
      "assessment": "Existing021 intentionally infers no13thnoteclass, ordinalencoding or unseenmatrixpixels/default; only actualC→D/UIroles selected.",
      "documentary_expected": null,
      "documentary_comparison": null,
      "actual_expected": null,
      "actual_comparison": null,
      "target": "U",
      "executed": false,
      "complete": false,
      "quotes": [
        {
          "block": "NME:B092",
          "json_pointer": "/blocks/91/text",
          "quote": "The Note Matrix uses a 13x13 grid where each row and column corresponds to the 12 notes in a full octave. The columns represent incoming note values, while the rows show their outgoing equivalents."
        },
        {
          "block": "NME:B093",
          "json_pointer": "/blocks/92/text",
          "quote": "The black squares correspond to the black keys on a keyboard. The highlighted squares determine how each note is mapped based on the set scale. The scale mapping begins from the root note, located in the bottom left corner of the Note Matrix. You can set the root note using the Base chooser and select a scale using the Scale Name chooser. Alternatively, you can enable the Use Current Scale toggle in the device title bar to apply the clip’s root note and scale. Once enabled, the Base and Scale Name choosers are deactivated, as the clip defines these settings."
        },
        {
          "block": "NME:B094",
          "json_pointer": "/blocks/93/text",
          "quote": "You can create your own scale by selecting User from the Scale Name chooser. By default, each highlighted square is mapped to its corresponding note within an octave — C outputs C, C# outputs C#, and so on. You can move highlighted squares with the mouse or delete them with mouse clicks to customize which notes are included in the scale. Removing a note from the Note Matrix means that note will no longer be output, even if played."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
